IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `ccc-core`
IMPORT `ccc30-forms-actors`

-- ccc30-forms-appearance.l4 -- FORM 6, 6.2, 6.3, 9, 10, 10.1, 11, 11.1 and 12 of the Criminal Code (Canada), R.S.C., 1985, c. C-46 (raw text lines 17947-18001 and 18074-18326):
-- the summons, the appearance notice, the undertaking, the release order, the orders to appear for the Identification of Criminals Act and for section 490.0132,
-- the witness undertaking and the surety declaration: what a person who is handed one of them is told, and what the text says follows.
-- Source: a plain-text rendering, made by a converter script, of the Justice Laws Website consolidation (current to 2026-09-30); it is not the
-- official text and nothing here is claimed verified against the official text.
-- WHAT IS A RULE HERE. The identification, contact, charge and signature blocks, the blank conditions and the application (6.1) are prescribed wording. What is encoded is what
-- the Forms say to the person: the duty to attend or appear; the warnings (an offence, a warrant for arrest, cancellation, detention, forfeiture); the exception that an
-- inaccurate description is not a lawful excuse; the limits on the conditions that may be checked ($500, 200 km, "reasonable ... and necessary"); how the conditions may be varied
-- and how long they last; that the person need not accept them and what follows if they do not; and what a surety may do.
-- THE OFFENCES AND THE WARRANTS ARE NOT HERE. "An offence under subsection 145(3)", "a warrant for your arrest (section 512)" and "cancelled ... detained (section 524)" are what the FORM
-- tells the person; the sections themselves are another part's. Every function here is named for the Form and answers what the Form says (NOTES.md, Seams).
-- How a result reads: JUST TRUE; JUST FALSE (the text says no in its own words); NOTHING (not covered). Every JUST FALSE branch carries a FALSE-kind comment that quotes the words;
-- every NOTHING branch carries a NOTHING-kind comment. A caller must never turn NOTHING into FALSE.

§ `Forms 6 to 12 -- the vocabulary the warnings share`

-- What the warnings in Forms 6, 6.2, 6.3, 9, 10, 10.1 and 11 say may follow an offence under section 145: "a warrant for your arrest may be issued ... and you may be liable to a fine or
-- to imprisonment, or to both".
DECLARE `forms 6 to 11.1 consequence of the offence` IS ONE OF
    `forms 6 to 11.1 a warrant for the arrest of the person may be issued`
    `forms 6 to 11.1 the person may be liable to a fine`
    `forms 6 to 11.1 the person may be liable to imprisonment`
    `forms 6 to 11.1 the person may be liable to both a fine and imprisonment`
    `forms 6 to 11.1 another consequence`

-- What Form 11.1 says may follow "If you do not comply with this order": "you may be arrested and the release order to which you are subject may be cancelled and, as a result, you may be detained in custody".
DECLARE `form 11.1 consequence of not complying` IS ONE OF
    `form 11.1 the person may be arrested`
    `form 11.1 the release order may be cancelled`
    `form 11.1 the person may be detained in custody as a result`
    `form 11.1 another consequence`

-- The duties the Forms put on the person, as actions for the regulative rules (the actor is the core's `the accused`, `the offender` or `a witness`).
DECLARE `forms 6 to 11.1 action` IS ONE OF
    `form 6 attends court as ordered`
    `form 6.2 appears for the purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act`
    `form 6.3 appears for the purposes of considering an order under subsection 490.012(1) to (3)`
    `form 9 attends court as required`
    `form 10 attends court as required`
    `form 10.1 attends court as required`
    `form 11.1 appears for the purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act`

§ `Form 6 -- Summons to a person charged with an offence`

-- "(a) to appear on (date) at (hour) at (place) for the purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act (Ignore, if not filled in); and"
-- "Ignore, if not filled in": an "if". Filled in, the instruction does not apply (JUST FALSE).
-- src:17954 | (a) to appear on (date) at (hour) at (place) for the purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act (Ignore, if not filled in); and
@ref Form 6 (Section 2) Summons to a Person Charged with an Offence, paragraph (a) (src:17954)
GIVEN `paragraph (a) is filled in` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`form 6 paragraph (a) is to be ignored` MEANS
    IF `paragraph (a) is filled in`
    THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Ignore, if not filled in"
    ELSE JUST TRUE

-- (b) to attend court on (date), at (hour), at (place), or before any justice for the (territorial division) who is there, and to attend court at any time after as required by the court, in order to be dealt with according to law.
-- The days until the day set out are the input. After the first attendance the duty continues "at any time after as required by the court": a standing duty with no date, not modelled here.
-- src:17955 | (b) to attend court on (date), at (hour), at (place), or before any justice for the (territorial division) who is there, and to attend court at any time after as required by the court, in order to be dealt with according to law.
@ref Form 6 (Section 2) Summons to a Person Charged with an Offence, the duty to attend or appear (src:17955)
GIVEN `the days until the day set out in the document` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party `forms 6 to 11.1 action`
`form 6 the accused must attend court` MEANS
    PARTY `the accused`
    MUST `form 6 attends court as ordered`
    WITHIN `the days until the day set out in the document`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the accused`
         BECAUSE "Form 6: the person did not attend or appear as the Form requires (the Form warns of the offence, the warrant for arrest and the cancellation it describes)"

-- You are warned that, unless you have a lawful excuse, it is an offence under subsection 145(3) of the Criminal Code to fail to appear for the purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act or to attend court, as required in this summons.
-- A person who did not fail to appear for the purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act or to attend court, as required in the summons is outside the sentence's words (NOTHING); a lawful excuse is "unless you have a lawful excuse" (JUST FALSE, once met: NOTES.md F-B).
-- src:17956 | You are warned that, unless you have a lawful excuse, it is an offence under subsection 145(3) of the Criminal Code to fail to appear for the purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act or to attend court, as required in this summons.
@ref Form 6 (Section 2) Summons to a Person Charged with an Offence, the warning of the offence (src:17956)
GIVEN `the person failed to appear for the purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act or to attend court, as required in the summons` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person has a lawful excuse` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`form 6 warning: unless the person has a lawful excuse, failing to appear for the purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act or to attend court, as required in the summons is an offence under subsection 145(3)` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the person failed to appear for the purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act or to attend court, as required in the summons`
        THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "to fail to appear for the purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act or to attend court, as required in this summons"
        IF `the person has a lawful excuse`
        THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "unless you have a lawful excuse"
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- If you commit an offence under subsection 145(3) of the Criminal Code, a warrant for your arrest may be issued (Section 512 or 512.1 of the Criminal Code) and you may be liable to a fine or to imprisonment, or to both.
-- "If" unmet: JUST FALSE. A consequence the sentence does not list: not covered (the list is not closed with "only").
-- src:17957 | If you commit an offence under subsection 145(3) of the Criminal Code, a warrant for your arrest may be issued (Section 512 or 512.1 of the Criminal Code) and you may be liable to a fine or to imprisonment, or to both.
@ref Form 6 (Section 2) Summons to a Person Charged with an Offence, what may follow an offence (src:17957)
GIVEN `the person commits an offence under subsection 145(3)` IS A BOOLEAN
      consequence IS A `forms 6 to 11.1 consequence of the offence`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`form 6 if the person commits an offence under subsection 145(3), the Form states that` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the person commits an offence under subsection 145(3)`
        THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "If you commit an offence under subsection 145(3) of the Criminal Code"
        OTHERWISE
            CONSIDER consequence
            WHEN `forms 6 to 11.1 a warrant for the arrest of the person may be issued` THEN JUST TRUE
            WHEN `forms 6 to 11.1 the person may be liable to a fine` THEN JUST TRUE
            WHEN `forms 6 to 11.1 the person may be liable to imprisonment` THEN JUST TRUE
            WHEN `forms 6 to 11.1 the person may be liable to both a fine and imprisonment` THEN JUST TRUE
            WHEN `forms 6 to 11.1 another consequence` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (list not closed): the sentence names a warrant for arrest and "you may be liable to a fine or to imprisonment, or to both", and does not say "only"

-- If you do not comply with this summons or are charged with committing an offence after it has been issued to you, this summons may be cancelled and, as a result, you may be detained in custody (subsections 524(4) and (6.2) of the Criminal Code).
-- "If you do not comply ... or are charged ...": either limb suffices. "may be cancelled" is a power limited by its "If": neither limb met is NOTHING (fork F-W; the other reading, a failed "If" as the text's own no, is JUST FALSE).
-- "as a result, you may be detained" is a result of the cancellation only.
-- src:17958 | If you do not comply with this summons or are charged with committing an offence after it has been issued to you, this summons may be cancelled and, as a result, you may be detained in custody (subsections 524(4) and (6.2) of the Criminal Code).
@ref Form 6 (Section 2) Summons to a Person Charged with an Offence, cancellation and detention (src:17958)
GIVEN `the person does not comply with the summons` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is charged with committing an offence after the summons was issued to the person` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`form 6 the summons may be cancelled` MEANS
    IF `the person does not comply with the summons` OR `the person is charged with committing an offence after the summons was issued to the person`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "If you do not comply with this summons or are charged with committing an offence after it has been issued to you"

-- src:17958 | If you do not comply with this summons or are charged with committing an offence after it has been issued to you, this summons may be cancelled and, as a result, you may be detained in custody (subsections 524(4) and (6.2) of the Criminal Code).
@ref Form 6 (Section 2) Summons to a Person Charged with an Offence, cancellation and detention (src:17958)
GIVEN `the summons has been cancelled` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`form 6 the person may be detained in custody as a result of the cancellation` MEANS
    IF `the summons has been cancelled`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "as a result, you may be detained in custody"

§ `Form 6.2 -- Summons to appear for the purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act`

-- You are ordered, in Her Majesty’s name, to appear on (date) at (hour) at (place) for the purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act in relation to (set out briefly the offence ...).
-- The actor is the core's `the accused`: the Form is addressed to "(name of person)" previously required to appear as an accused or an offender (a reading: NOTES.md F-I).
-- src:17982 | You are ordered, in Her Majesty’s name, to appear on (date) at (hour) at (place) for the purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act in relation to (set out briefly the offence in respect of which the accused was charged or the offender has been determined to be guilty).
@ref Form 6.2 (Subsection 485.2(1)) Summons to Appear for the Purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act, the duty to attend or appear (src:17982)
GIVEN `the days until the day set out in the document` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party `forms 6 to 11.1 action`
`form 6.2 the person must appear for the purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act` MEANS
    PARTY `the accused`
    MUST `form 6.2 appears for the purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act`
    WITHIN `the days until the day set out in the document`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the accused`
         BECAUSE "Form 6.2: the person did not attend or appear as the Form requires (the Form warns of the offence, the warrant for arrest and the cancellation it describes)"

-- You are warned that, unless you have a lawful excuse, it is an offence under subsection 145(3) of the Criminal Code to fail to appear for the purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act, as required in this summons.
-- A person who did not fail to appear for the purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act, as required in the summons is outside the sentence's words (NOTHING); a lawful excuse is "unless you have a lawful excuse" (JUST FALSE, once met: NOTES.md F-B).
