@ref Form 6.2 (Subsection 485.2(1)) Summons to Appear for the Purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act, the warning of the offence (src:17983)
GIVEN `the person failed to appear for the purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act, as required in the summons` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person has a lawful excuse` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`form 6.2 warning: unless the person has a lawful excuse, failing to appear for the purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act, as required in the summons is an offence under subsection 145(3)` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the person failed to appear for the purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act, as required in the summons`
        THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "to fail to appear for the purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act, as required in this summons"
        IF `the person has a lawful excuse`
        THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "unless you have a lawful excuse"
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- If you commit an offence under subsection 145(3) of the Criminal Code, a warrant for your arrest may be issued (section 512 or 512.1 of the Criminal Code) and you may be liable to a fine or to imprisonment, or to both.
-- "If" unmet: JUST FALSE. A consequence the sentence does not list: not covered (the list is not closed with "only").
-- src:17984 | If you commit an offence under subsection 145(3) of the Criminal Code, a warrant for your arrest may be issued (section 512 or 512.1 of the Criminal Code) and you may be liable to a fine or to imprisonment, or to both.
@ref Form 6.2 (Subsection 485.2(1)) Summons to Appear for the Purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act, what may follow an offence (src:17984)
GIVEN `the person commits an offence under subsection 145(3)` IS A BOOLEAN
      consequence IS A `forms 6 to 11.1 consequence of the offence`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`form 6.2 if the person commits an offence under subsection 145(3), the Form states that` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the person commits an offence under subsection 145(3)`
        THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "If you commit an offence under subsection 145(3) of the Criminal Code"
        OTHERWISE
            CONSIDER consequence
            WHEN `forms 6 to 11.1 a warrant for the arrest of the person may be issued` THEN JUST TRUE
            WHEN `forms 6 to 11.1 the person may be liable to a fine` THEN JUST TRUE
            WHEN `forms 6 to 11.1 the person may be liable to imprisonment` THEN JUST TRUE
            WHEN `forms 6 to 11.1 the person may be liable to both a fine and imprisonment` THEN JUST TRUE
            WHEN `forms 6 to 11.1 another consequence` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (list not closed): the sentence names a warrant for arrest and "you may be liable to a fine or to imprisonment, or to both", and does not say "only"

-- "If you are on interim release and do not comply with this summons or are charged with committing an offence after it has been issued to you, any summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order to which
-- you are subject may be cancelled and, as a result, you may be detained in custody (section 524 of the Criminal Code)."
-- "may be cancelled" is a power limited by its "If": a person not on interim release, or with neither limb met, is outside it (NOTHING, fork F-W; the other reading, a failed "If" as the text's own no, is JUST FALSE).
-- src:17985 | If you are on interim release and do not comply with this summons or are charged with committing an offence after it has been issued to you, any summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order to which you are subject may be cancelled and, as a result, you may be detained in custody (section 524 of the Criminal Code).
@ref Form 6.2 (Subsection 485.2(1)) Summons to Appear for the Purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act, cancellation and detention (src:17985)
GIVEN `the person is on interim release` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person does not comply with the summons` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is charged with committing an offence after the summons was issued to the person` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`form 6.2 the summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order to which the person is subject may be cancelled` MEANS
    IF `the person is on interim release`
       AND (`the person does not comply with the summons` OR `the person is charged with committing an offence after the summons was issued to the person`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "If you are on interim release and do not comply with this summons or are charged with committing an offence after it has been issued to you"

-- src:17985 | If you are on interim release and do not comply with this summons or are charged with committing an offence after it has been issued to you, any summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order to which you are subject may be cancelled and, as a result, you may be detained in custody (section 524 of the Criminal Code).
@ref Form 6.2 (Subsection 485.2(1)) Summons to Appear for the Purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act, cancellation and detention (src:17985)
GIVEN `the document to which the person is subject has been cancelled` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`form 6.2 the person may be detained in custody as a result of the cancellation` MEANS
    IF `the document to which the person is subject has been cancelled`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "as a result, you may be detained in custody"

§ `Form 6.3 -- Summons to appear for the purposes of section 490.0132`

-- You are ordered, in His Majesty’s name, to appear on (date) at (hour) at (place) for the purposes of considering whether an order should be made under any of subsections 490.012(1) to (3) of the Criminal Code in respect of the primary offence ...
-- The actor is the core's `the offender`: a sentence was imposed, or a verdict of not criminally responsible rendered, for a primary offence (a reading: NOTES.md F-I). The Form says "His Majesty’s name" where the others say "Her Majesty’s" (NOTES.md finding).
-- src:17996 | You are ordered, in His Majesty’s name, to appear on (date) at (hour) at (place) for the purposes of considering whether an order should be made under any of subsections 490.012(1) to (3) of the Criminal Code in respect of the primary offence for which the sentence was imposed or the verdict of not criminally responsible on account of mental disorder was rendered.
@ref Form 6.3 (Section 490.0132) Summons to Appear for the Purposes of Section 490.0132, the duty to attend or appear (src:17996)
GIVEN `the days until the day set out in the document` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party `forms 6 to 11.1 action`
`form 6.3 the person must appear for the purposes of considering an order under section 490.012` MEANS
    PARTY `the offender`
    MUST `form 6.3 appears for the purposes of considering an order under subsection 490.012(1) to (3)`
    WITHIN `the days until the day set out in the document`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the offender`
         BECAUSE "Form 6.3: the person did not attend or appear as the Form requires (the Form warns of the offence, the warrant for arrest and the cancellation it describes)"

-- You are warned that, unless you have a lawful excuse, it is an offence under subsection 145(3) of the Criminal Code to fail to appear as required in this summons.
-- A person who did not fail to appear as required in the summons is outside the sentence's words (NOTHING); a lawful excuse is "unless you have a lawful excuse" (JUST FALSE, once met: NOTES.md F-B).
