§ `Form 9 -- Appearance notice`

-- You must attend court as indicated below, and afterwards as required by the court:
-- The days until the date, time and court indicated are the input; "afterwards as required by the court" is a standing duty with no date, not modelled here.
-- src:18086 | You must attend court as indicated below, and afterwards as required by the court:
@ref Form 9 (Section 2) Appearance Notice, the duty to attend or appear (src:18086)
GIVEN `the days until the day set out in the document` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party `forms 6 to 11.1 action`
`form 9 the accused must attend court` MEANS
    PARTY `the accused`
    MUST `form 9 attends court as required`
    WITHIN `the days until the day set out in the document`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the accused`
         BECAUSE "Form 9: the person did not attend or appear as the Form requires (the Form warns of the offence, the warrant for arrest and the cancellation it describes)"

-- "(a) in the case where charges have been laid against you, unless you have a lawful excuse, you commit an offence under subsection 145(3) of the Criminal Code if you fail to appear for the purposes of the
-- Identification of Criminals Act or to attend court, as required in this appearance notice;"
-- Three conditions, each the text's own: "in the case where charges have been laid", "if you fail to appear ... or to attend court", "unless you have a lawful excuse".
-- src:18095 | (a) in the case where charges have been laid against you, unless you have a lawful excuse, you commit an offence under subsection 145(3) of the Criminal Code if you fail to appear for the purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act or to attend court, as required in this appearance notice;
@ref Form 9 (Section 2) Appearance Notice, item 6(a) (src:18095)
GIVEN `charges have been laid against the person` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person failed to appear for the purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act or to attend court, as required in the appearance notice` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person has a lawful excuse` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`form 9 item 6(a) warning: where charges have been laid, unless the person has a lawful excuse, failing to appear or to attend court is an offence under subsection 145(3)` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `charges have been laid against the person`
        THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "in the case where charges have been laid against you"
        IF `the person has a lawful excuse`
        THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "unless you have a lawful excuse"
        IF NOT `the person failed to appear for the purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act or to attend court, as required in the appearance notice`
        THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if you fail to appear for the purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act or to attend court, as required in this appearance notice"
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- "(b) in the case where no charges have been laid against you and you fail to appear at a judicial referral hearing under section 523.1, as required in this appearance notice, charges may be laid against
-- you for the alleged offence described in item 3 of this notice."  "in the case where" is read as "where" (a failed condition is JUST FALSE: NOTES.md F-C).
-- src:18096 | (b) in the case where no charges have been laid against you and you fail to appear at a judicial referral hearing under section 523.1, as required in this appearance notice, charges may be laid against you for the alleged offence described in item 3 of this notice.
@ref Form 9 (Section 2) Appearance Notice, item 6(b) (src:18096)
GIVEN `no charges have been laid against the person` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person fails to appear at a judicial referral hearing under section 523.1, as required in the appearance notice` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`form 9 item 6(b) warning: charges may be laid against the person for the alleged offence` MEANS
    IF `no charges have been laid against the person` AND `the person fails to appear at a judicial referral hearing under section 523.1, as required in the appearance notice`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "in the case where no charges have been laid against you and you fail to appear at a judicial referral hearing under section 523.1, as required in this appearance notice"

-- If you commit an offence under subsection 145(3) of the Criminal Code, a warrant for your arrest may be issued (section 512 or 512.2 of the Criminal Code) and you may be liable to a fine or to imprisonment, or to both.
-- "If" unmet: JUST FALSE. A consequence the sentence does not list: not covered (the list is not closed with "only").
