    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "you or the prosecutor may apply to a judge"

-- "... remain in effect until ...": an "until" bar (JUST TRUE before the end, JUST FALSE once an ending event has happened).
-- src:18235 | The conditions indicated by a check mark on this release order (including any obligations imposed on your sureties) remain in effect until they are cancelled or changed or until you have been discharged, sentenced or otherwise detained by the court (sections 763 and 764 of the Criminal Code).
@ref Form 11 (Section 2) Release Order, how long the conditions remain in effect (src:18235)
GIVEN `the conditions have been cancelled or changed` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person has been discharged, sentenced or otherwise detained by the court` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`form 11 the conditions remain in effect` MEANS
    IF `the conditions have been cancelled or changed` OR `the person has been discharged, sentenced or otherwise detained by the court`
    THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "until they are cancelled or changed or until you have been discharged, sentenced or otherwise detained by the court"
    ELSE JUST TRUE

-- You are warned that, unless you have a lawful excuse, you commit an offence under section 145 of the Criminal Code if you fail to follow any of the conditions set out in this release order, including if you fail to attend court as required.
-- "if you fail to follow any of the conditions": an "if" (JUST FALSE when unmet); "unless you have a lawful excuse" (JUST FALSE, once met: NOTES.md F-B).
