      `the person has a lawful excuse` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`form 11.1 warning: unless the person has a lawful excuse, failing to appear for the purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act, as required by the order is an offence under subsection 145(2)` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the person failed to appear for the purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act, as required by the order`
        THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "to fail to appear for the purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act, as required by this order"
        IF `the person has a lawful excuse`
        THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "unless you have a lawful excuse"
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- If you commit an offence under subsection 145(2) of the Criminal Code, a warrant for your arrest may be issued (section 512, 512.2 or 512.3 of the Criminal Code) and you may be liable to a fine or to imprisonment, or to both.
-- "If" unmet: JUST FALSE. A consequence the sentence does not list: not covered (the list is not closed with "only").
